Two Pointers 
Idea: Use two indexes/pointers to traverse an array/string instead of using nested loops.

Usually:

int left = 0;
int right = nums.Length - 1;

while (left < right)
{
    // process nums[left] and nums[right]

    if (/* condition */)
        left++;
    else
        right--;
}

When to use
Look for these clues:

Sorted array
Find a pair satisfying a condition
Compare elements from both ends
Remove duplicates
Palindrome problems

Need O(n) instead of O(n²)

Classic example — Two Sum in sorted array

int left = 0;
int right = nums.Length - 1;

while (left < right)
{
    int sum = nums[left] + nums[right];

    if (sum == target)
        return new[] { left, right };

    if (sum < target)
        left++;
    else
        right--;
}

return Array.Empty<int>();

Why does this work?

For a sorted array:

[1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
 ↑              ↑
left           right

Sum too small → move left right
Sum too large → move right left
Sum correct → found it
Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)

Important patterns
1. Opposite directions

left →       ← right

Used for:

Two Sum II
Container With Most Water
Valid Palindrome
3Sum

2. Same direction / fast & slow

slow →
fast →→

Used for:

Remove duplicates
Move zeroes
Linked-list cycle detection
Partitioning
